WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.351 --> 00:00:02.309
This is the Transfers page.

2
00:00:02.309 --> 00:00:06.402
It brings together every deposit and withdrawal across your connected

3
00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:09.782
exchanges, and works out where your money actually went.

4
00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:14.044
Cyrus fetches this history from each exchange and keeps

5
00:00:14.044 --> 00:00:17.563
a copy on your computer, so the page opens instantly.

6
00:00:17.563 --> 00:00:20.728
Your first visit pulls everything automatically.

7
00:00:20.728 --> 00:00:24.478
After that, the refresh button fetches anything new.

8
00:00:25.151 --> 00:00:28.851
If an exchange can't provide this history, Cyrus says so.

9
00:00:28.851 --> 00:00:33.173
Robinhood doesn't offer it, so nothing from Robinhood appears here.

10
00:00:33.173 --> 00:00:37.574
A key that's missing the permission to read funding history gets a notice too.

11
00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:40.163
The page opens on the Roadmap.

12
00:00:40.163 --> 00:00:44.592
It shows each movement once, newest first and grouped by day, with where

13
00:00:44.592 --> 00:00:48.528
the money came from on the left and where it went on the right.

14
00:00:49.224 --> 00:00:54.733
A red edge means money left one of your exchanges, and green means money arrived.

15
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:58.286
The summary line counts what's been paired, what's waiting

16
00:00:58.286 --> 00:01:01.900
for your review, and what has an end Cyrus can't identify.

17
00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.804
When you move coins between exchanges, one reports a withdrawal

18
00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:10.694
and the other reports a deposit, as two unrelated events.

19
00:01:10.694 --> 00:01:14.598
Cyrus joins them back together, so this move from Binance

20
00:01:14.598 --> 00:01:17.955
to Kraken shows as one hop with both ends named.

21
00:01:18.613 --> 00:01:21.991
Same transaction means both exchanges reported the

22
00:01:21.991 --> 00:01:25.504
same on-chain transaction, so the match is certain.

23
00:01:25.504 --> 00:01:28.940
The small amount lost on the way is shown as the network

24
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.701
fee, with how long the coins took to arrive.

25
00:01:32.381 --> 00:01:36.999
Without a shared transaction, Cyrus compares the amount and the timing.

26
00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:41.888
It looks for the same coin on two different exchanges, with the deposit landing

27
00:01:41.888 --> 00:01:46.653
within three days and no more than a few percent smaller, to allow for fees.

28
00:01:46.653 --> 00:01:48.794
The result is shown as a score.

29
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:54.048
Cyrus only pairs these by itself when it's at least ninety-five percent

30
00:01:54.048 --> 00:01:57.917
sure, and no other transfer could plausibly be the partner.

31
00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:01.348
When candidates compete, it leaves the decision to you.

32
00:02:02.046 --> 00:02:04.935
Anything less certain becomes a question for you.

33
00:02:04.935 --> 00:02:09.716
This Tether withdrawal from Kraken looks like the same movement as a slightly

34
00:02:09.716 --> 00:02:14.124
smaller deposit on Binance, and Cyrus is ninety-one percent confident.

35
00:02:14.794 --> 00:02:16.486
If it's right, click Same.

36
00:02:16.486 --> 00:02:21.712
The pair is confirmed, and locked so that later syncs never undo your decision.

37
00:02:22.387 --> 00:02:24.544
If it's wrong, click Not the same.

38
00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:28.436
Cyrus remembers that answer, so it won't suggest the pair again,

39
00:02:28.436 --> 00:02:31.659
and both transfers are free to match something else.

40
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.178
This matters for your taxes.

41
00:02:34.178 --> 00:02:40.349
A confirmed pair carries the coins' original cost and purchase date over to the new exchange.

42
00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:43.830
Leave it unconfirmed, and the deposit on Binance looks

43
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.731
like coins from outside, with no known cost.

44
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.494
Some transfers have only one side in your exchange data, because the other end is a wallet.

45
00:02:53.494 --> 00:02:56.439
When you've labelled that wallet, the hop names it,

46
00:02:56.439 --> 00:02:59.385
like this Bitcoin sent from Coinbase to My Ledger.

47
00:03:00.052 --> 00:03:02.582
A labelled wallet doesn't have to be yours.

48
00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:08.459
This rent payment goes to someone else's address, shown with a person icon instead of a wallet.

49
00:03:09.143 --> 00:03:12.138
And when Cyrus doesn't know, it says so.

50
00:03:12.138 --> 00:03:17.205
This deposit came with no address, so it reads Unknown source rather than guessing.

51
00:03:17.205 --> 00:03:21.128
For a genuine payment from outside, there's nothing to fix.

52
00:03:21.798 --> 00:03:26.682
When the exchange did report an address, the unknown end becomes a button.

53
00:03:26.682 --> 00:03:28.796
Click name it to label that address.

54
00:03:29.466 --> 00:03:33.831
That opens Tracked wallets, with the address already filled in.

55
00:03:33.831 --> 00:03:40.600
Label an address once, and every transfer to or from it is attributed, past and future.

56
00:03:41.293 --> 00:03:43.198
Give it a name you'll recognise.

57
00:03:43.198 --> 00:03:50.126
The chain, a memo or tag for coins like Stellar and XRP, the asset, and notes are all optional.

58
00:03:50.803 --> 00:03:53.918
Leave This wallet is mine ticked for your own wallets.

59
00:03:53.918 --> 00:03:58.080
Only those turn a transfer into a move between your own accounts.

60
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.976
Untick it for someone else's address that you just want to recognise.

61
00:04:01.976 --> 00:04:03.426
Then click Add wallet.

62
00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:09.245
Seen in your transfers lists addresses already in your history that aren't labelled yet.

63
00:04:09.245 --> 00:04:11.838
Click one and it fills in the form for you.

64
00:04:11.838 --> 00:04:13.740
It's the quickest way to get started.

65
00:04:14.419 --> 00:04:17.933
With more than a few wallets, use Add several at once.

66
00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:22.454
Download the template, fill in one row per wallet, and import it back.

67
00:04:22.454 --> 00:04:26.829
Cyrus previews what will be added or skipped before anything is saved.

68
00:04:26.829 --> 00:04:30.672
Export saved gives you your current list in the same format.

69
00:04:31.345 --> 00:04:37.146
Saved wallets lists everything you've labelled, and how many transfers each one explains.

70
00:04:37.146 --> 00:04:39.234
You can edit or remove them here.

71
00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:43.316
Removing one returns its transfers to an unknown address.

72
00:04:43.994 --> 00:04:46.537
For the raw records, switch to Table.

73
00:04:46.537 --> 00:04:51.749
Each deposit and withdrawal is a row, with the exchange, the type, the

74
00:04:51.749 --> 00:04:57.109
coin, the amount and fee, the status, the time, and the transaction ID.

75
00:04:57.792 --> 00:05:02.064
Status shows whether a transfer is complete, pending, or failed.

76
00:05:02.064 --> 00:05:05.680
Failed and canceled transfers never moved any money, so

77
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.165
they're never matched or counted in your cost basis.

78
00:05:09.165 --> 00:05:13.500
Internal marks a move between your own accounts on the same exchange.

79
00:05:14.224 --> 00:05:18.326
The filters narrow the table to one exchange account, or to just

80
00:05:18.326 --> 00:05:21.980
deposits or withdrawals, and the count updates to match.

81
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:25.038
Long histories are split into pages of a hundred.

82
00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:29.030
The asset filter works in both views.

83
00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:34.695
Back on the Roadmap, choose Bitcoin, and you can trace exactly where your Bitcoin has been.

84
00:05:35.375 --> 00:05:36.615
That's Transfers.

85
00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:42.479
Keep your history synced, answer the matches Cyrus suggests, and label the wallets you own.

86
00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:47.998
Every movement you settle here makes the cost basis on the Taxes page more complete.

